## What

SQL Injection vulnerability is a vulnerability where a user can make the SQL interpreter to read malicious input from an input file or another external source and insert it into the query. This can lead to SQL syntax errors and information disclosure attacks.

## Where

This bug may occur in applications which allow the use of a variety of SQL commands. This includes all major relational databases such as SQLite, MySQL, MariaDB, SQL Server, AWS Aurora and many more.

For example, this [article](https://www.sqlshack.com/sql-injection-explained/) provides a more detailed explanation.

## When

There are a variety of ways in which this bug can occur. Some of these examples include a SQL database table column or the SQL query itself. These can be identified using either source code or database queries with UNION injection.

In order to test this vulnerability, one would need to take over the application by running a database command to provide a fake result.

## Severity

This is not a security-critical vulnerability, however it is a very severe one as a code execution vulnerability.

## Steps

The easiest way to test this vulnerability is by creating a SQL query which concatenates 2 strings with the `+` character. This is done to prevent SQL logic.


Example:
```java
String query = "SELECT * FROM user WHERE name like " + userInput;
```

Next, if one checks the query with SQL syntax, it should be apparent of the logic error. But, when this logic is vulnerable and executed, it can be used for a SQL Injection attack.

