import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class ClassX {

    /**
     * Parses an HTTP header.
     * 
     * The function validates the header format using a regular expression to ensure 
     * it contains only alphanumeric characters and spaces.
     */
    void validate(String header, String name) {
        String check = "^[A-Z]|[a-z]|\\s+$";
        Matcher headerValidator = Pattern.compile(check).matcher(header);
        if (headerValidator.find()) {
            System.out.println(String.format("%s: Valid Input for %s", name, header));
        } else {
            System.out.println(String.format("%s: Invalid Input for %s\n%1$s: %2$s", name, header));
        }
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {

        ClassX classX = new ClassX();
        classX.validate("1234", "header-value");
        classX.validate("INVALID123", "header-value");

    }

}

-----
The regex for the header is "^[A-Z]|[a-z]\\s+$", which matches any capital letter, one or more lowercase letters followed by one or more whitespace. The function first checks if the input matches the regex pattern, and prints 'Valid Header' if the input is valid. If the input is not valid, the function prints 'Invalid Header' and the header format. Then the function prints out the header along with its name.

